<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clayton–Bulwer Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1850</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Clayton–Bulwer Treaty was signed in 1850 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, represented by John M. Clayton and Sir Henry Bulwer. Both powers agreed that neither would seek exclusive control of a future canal across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> or colonise any part of the region. Disputes over its meaning led Britain to give up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a>, and it was replaced by the Hay–Pauncefote Treaty of 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clayton-bulwer-treaty.jpg" alt="Clayton–Bulwer Treaty" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Fagnani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Fagnani_(1819-1873)_-_William_Bulwer,_Baron_Dalling_and_Bulwer_-_NPG_852_-_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
